			<h1>What is a Drill Metre actually worth?</h1>
			<p class="subtitle">Why digitising legacy geological data is only the beginning</p>

			<p>Exploration likes visible progress: reports digitised, targets generated, models built and metres drilled. These are all easy to count. They tell us what a team did, but not necessarily what it learned.</p>
			<p>The metric I care about is <strong>cost per unit of decision-relevant geological uncertainty removed</strong>.</p>
			<p>This matters as explorers and governments revisit decades of historical geological records. Modern data systems can make an archive searchable at a scale that would once have been impractical. They cannot make an unreliable observation reliable simply by putting it into a database.</p>

			<h2>Digitisation is the starting point</h2>
			<p>KoBold Metals' work in Zambia is a useful public example of digitisation at scale. In partnership with Zambia's Ministry of Mines and Minerals Development and SMART Zambia, KoBold supported the Geological Survey Department's Digital Store. Its public account describes more than 100,000 pages of maps, reports and mineral data being processed into an accessible archive (<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/koboldmetals_decades-of-zambias-geological-history-are-activity-7375970731261829120-Rc70" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">KoBold Metals</a>).</p>
			<p>KoBold describes its wider data system as organising reports, legacy maps, handwritten notes, geophysical data and geochemical data into a standardised, searchable form across languages (<a href="https://koboldmetals.com/science/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">KoBold Metals: Science</a>). That sort of infrastructure can preserve knowledge, reduce duplicated work and make exploration data easier to access.</p>
			<p>But accessible does not mean decision-ready. A scanned report is preserved; a translated report is readable; a structured record is searchable. A separate question remains: how much confidence should we place in what it says?</p>

			<h2>A historical archive is a collection of claims</h2>
			<p>I would not treat a legacy archive as one dataset that is either reliable or unreliable. I would treat it as a collection of claims.</p>
			<p>A collar coordinate claims that a hole began at a particular location. A downhole survey claims that we know its path below surface. A lithology log claims that particular units were intersected. Assays, density measurements and historical resource estimates make different claims again. Each can fail differently.</p>
			<p>A collar may have been surveyed accurately in its original coordinate system but positioned incorrectly during a modern transformation. Drilling method, recovery, sample size, downhole deviation, assay coverage and detection limits each affect confidence in different ways. Geological logging adds another problem: interpretation is subjective.</p>
			<p>I see this even in modern operations. At one site I currently work with, more than five geologists can be logging during a single swing while several rigs drill broadly the same stratigraphy. The quantitative data is well assessed and standardised, yet interpretations of the same units can still vary significantly between geologists.</p>
			<p>The answer is not endless new forms and approvals. It is consistent training, shared reference material and a system that highlights where logging begins to diverge, without creating unnecessary bureaucracy for the people doing the work. If that problem exists with modern tools, common standards and active supervision, it should be assumed to exist in historical campaigns too—probably to a greater degree.</p>
			<p>The context in which the data was collected also matters. Who collected it? What question were they trying to answer? Which commodities or outcomes did the organisation prioritise? What equipment and analytical methods were available? Why did apparently prospective ground remain undeveloped?</p>
			<p>Decades of non-development may reflect infrastructure, metallurgy, economics, politics or a different exploration thesis. It does not necessarily mean poor geology, but it is still evidence that needs explaining.</p>

			<h2>A Claim–Confidence–Test framework</h2>
			<p>My instinct would be to assess legacy data through three questions.</p>

			<h3>1. What does the record claim?</h3>
			<p>Capture the geological statement being made, not just the words or numbers in the report. Is it claiming continuity, grade, alteration, structure, mineralogy or the existence of a wider mineral system?</p>

			<h3>2. How much confidence should we place in it?</h3>
			<p>Assess confidence by data category, source and campaign. Keep the original provenance and expose contradictions rather than hiding them inside a cleaned dataset. Confidence should change when records are reconciled or new evidence arrives.</p>

			<h3>3. What is the cheapest modern test that could prove or disprove it?</h3>
			<p>The answer will not always be another drill hole. It could be a control-point transformation, selective collar resurvey, blind relogging, re-assaying retained material, modern geophysics or a carefully positioned twin hole.</p>
			<p>Complexity is useful only when it can change a target ranking or decision. The practical question is: what do we need to learn next, and what is the cheapest safe way to learn it?</p>

			<h2>Metres drilled are not an exploration outcome</h2>
			<p>I have always found it odd that exploration companies celebrate metres of diamond core or RC drilling as though footage is inherently valuable. Metres measure activity and capital consumption. They do not tell us whether the holes were well designed.</p>
			<p>In resource definition, success should mean improved geological confidence and, where justified, movement from Inferred towards Indicated or Measured. The <a href="https://www.jorc.org/docs/JORC_code_2012.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JORC Code</a> orders those Mineral Resource categories by increasing geological confidence.</p>
			<p>Before a Mineral Resource exists, the equivalent question is whether each hole strengthens, changes or kills the geological thesis. A short hole that disproves a bad model early can create more value than 10,000 metres confirming what was already assumed.</p>
			<p>Metres still matter for budgets, productivity and programme delivery. They are simply a weak measure of geological progress.</p>
			<p>Historical data should not remain frozen either. Ground overlooked decades ago may deserve another look after changes in economics, processing technology, infrastructure or geological understanding. That does not make an old occurrence newly viable; it means the original claim may justify a new question or test.</p>

			<h2>The system still has to reach the ground</h2>
			<p>Digital analysis can be quick. Field validation cannot always be, and the handover between the two is where value is often lost.</p>
			<p>The exploration loop runs from planning through earthworks, drilling, QA/QC and modelling to a decision. A promising target is not useful if the field programme cannot test it safely and efficiently. Equally, fast drilling creates problems if logging, sampling and interpretation cannot keep pace, leaving rigs or crews waiting for the next decision.</p>
			<p>A useful system should connect archive assessment, target generation and field validation. It should update confidence as new observations arrive and stop spending when a hypothesis no longer deserves another test.</p>
			<p>For me, that is the real measure of exploration performance: did the work remove enough uncertainty to improve the next decision, and was it worth what we spent to learn it?</p>
			<p>I would be interested to hear how others measure information gain in exploration.</p>
		</main>

